# Claude Supercharger — line-ending policy (WINDOWS-SUPPORT-PLAN G5)
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. A `.sh` file with CRLF does not run under Git Bash: the shebang is
# read as `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and the error names a program nobody typed —
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory". Every hook would fail at once,
# on a machine where nothing looks misconfigured.
#
# These rules are checkout-time policy, so they protect a Windows clone even
# though the repository itself has never contained a CRLF file (verified: 0).
#
# Executables and anything an interpreter reads MUST stay LF.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Data and config read by jq/python at runtime — a stray CR ends up inside a
# parsed string value rather than failing loudly.
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Prose. LF keeps diffs clean across platforms; nothing here is executed.
*.md    text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one place CRLF is the native convention. Nothing in this
# repo ships .ps1 today; the rule exists so a future Windows helper does not
# silently inherit the LF default.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary assets — never touched.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
